- Department: Students in technical or academic-heavy departments (e.g., CSE, SWE, Pharmacy, EEE, Civil) are more likely to read academic books most of the time; students in Arts or Business (e.g., BBA, English, JMC) may have more varied reading habits.
- Amount of time spent reading books per day: Positively correlated; more hours usually indicate reading academic books more often.
- Reason for reading books: If the reason is "Academic Purpose," likely the student reads academic books most of the time.
- How to manage books for reading: Borrowing or buying physical books might indicate more academic reading; E-books also common but not decisive alone.
- Number of e-books read last two years: Higher numbers likely indicate more academic reading if combined with other features.
- Number of printed books read last two years: Higher numbers indicate more academic reading.
- Favorite books for leisure time: If non-fiction or politics or biography, possibly more academic tendencies; fiction or others may indicate less academic focus.
- What change felt after reading books: "Increase Academic Performance" or "Increase Problem Solving Skill" suggests academic reading; "Feel Relax" suggests leisure reading.
